/*****************************************************************************
    printFamilyNew -- THE MOVING HALF of the print-family POP.
    Grammar minion round 2, 2026-07-30.

    ############################################################
    ##  THIS FIXTURE IS RED ON PURPOSE. IT IS NOT BROKEN.     ##
    ##  ITS TARGETS ARE `.divergence` FILES, NOT `.target`    ##
    ##  FILES, AND THEY RECORD THE **WRONG** ANSWER, PINNED.  ##
    ############################################################

    Same shape as genLadder/iterT1m.divergence and genLadder/tree.divergence:
    a fixture on an OPEN item, asserting that today's wrong answer is
    UNCHANGED. It goes RED when Tony's C++ change lands, and THAT IS THE
    ACCEPTANCE TEST. Whoever moves it accounts for the move -- and the header
    of each section below says, row by row, what the new answer must be.

    The green half is `incant/printFamily`: `print` and `string` only, no
    graft, byte-exact, and it must stay green THROUGH the change. Read that
    file's header for why the fixture is split in two.

    WHAT THE CHANGE IS
    ------------------
    kantCorpus CLAIM KANT-13 (COMPLETED by Tony 2026-07-30) closes the print
    family at four keywords over one PrintXP mechanism:

        keyword                    destination   divertible
        print                      stdout        YES (printTO)
        cout                       stdout        no
        cerr                       stderr        no
        string (and omitted form)  a value       n/a

    ONE spacing default across all four; `$` is the only mode that changes
    it; `_` is the explicit space. No per-destination spacing defaults.

    THE LOAD-BEARING ROW, AND IT IS SECTION 3
    -----------------------------------------
    kantCorpus CLAIM KANT-23: DIVERSION BELONGS TO `print` ALONE, and that is
    the ENTIRE REASON `cout` EXISTS. With a `printTO` diversion armed,
    `print` goes to the buffer and `cout` MUST STILL REACH THE TERMINAL.
    From source `cout` reads as a redundant second spelling of `print`; only
    that rationale makes it load-bearing. Section 3 is the only row in the
    project that tests it, and today it is WRONG -- see CLAIM GRAM-10.

    THE GRAFT, AND WHY IT IS SCAFFOLDING AND NOT THE THING UNDER TEST
    -----------------------------------------------------------------
    `cout` and `cerr` do not exist natively yet. Round 1 (CLAIM GRAM-3) found
    they can be grafted into the live WardeD at runtime with no shared file
    touched. The graft is here for ONE reason: WITHOUT IT THIS FILE DOES NOT
    RUN. An unmatched keyword abandons the rest of the file and still exits 0
    (CLAIM GRAM-8), so an ungrafted `cout` on line 1 of the matrix deletes
    every row after it, silently, at exit 0.

    ⚠ THE GRAFTED `cout` IS `print` WEARING A DIFFERENT KEYWORD. It reaches
    the same opPrint and is therefore DIVERTIBLE, which is exactly the
    property `cout` must NOT have. So the graft can never make section 3
    green, and it is not trying to. It makes sections 1-2 show the exact
    bytes native `cout` must produce, and it makes section 3 show, in one
    line, the thing that is wrong.

    `cerr` is NOT scaffolded to stdout, and that asymmetry is deliberate:
    scaffolding cout to stdout points at cout's REAL destination, whereas
    scaffolding cerr to stdout would be a lie about where the bytes go. Its
    label tag starts with 'c', so it mis-sinks to opString and the text is
    silently discarded (CLAIM GRAM-2). Silence is the honest pin: there is no
    stderr sink, and the bytes are lost.

    WHAT HAPPENS TO THE GRAFT WHEN NATIVE `cout` LANDS -- MEASURED
    --------------------------------------------------------------
    It becomes INERT, and this file does not need editing to survive the
    transition. A runtime graft lands at the TAIL of WardeD, and an EARLIER
    WardeD alternative WINS the match (CLAIM GRAM-11, run). A native rule
    defined in incant/grammar sits inside WardeD ahead of any tail graft, so
    once `cout`/`cerr` are native THE NATIVE RULES ANSWER and the graft is
    shadowed dead weight. The divergence files therefore flip because the
    IMPLEMENTATION changed, not because the fixture was rewritten.

    The rule names below are `Pf...Graft`, not `CoutXP`/`CerrXP`, so that a
    native rule cannot be silently RE-OPENED and clobbered by these defines.

    ⚠ WHEN YOU FLIP THIS FIXTURE GREEN, DELETE THE GRAFT BLOCK (down to the
    guard) IN THE SAME COMMIT. It is inert, not harmless-forever: it names
    `pCout`, and CLAIM GRAM-2's DEMOTED block is explicit that the `'p'`
    first-character hack dies with the fix and must not be preserved or
    designed around.

    TARGETS
        genLadder/printFamilyNew.divergence      stdout  -- WRONG, pinned
        genLadder/printFamilyNew.err.divergence  stderr  -- WRONG, pinned
*****************************************************************************/
Start();
search reset Grokking list;
register(PrintFamilyNew);

/*  ---- SCAFFOLDING GRAFT. DELETE WHEN cout/cerr GO NATIVE. ----
    Two defines, not one: a bare reference to a method-bearing field INVOKES
    it, so `WardeD += PfCoutGraft;` with ruleMethod already attached calls
    aCTionPrinT and adds a number instead of grafting the rule. Define plain,
    graft, then re-open to attach the method. CLAIM GRAM-1.                 */
define
    PfCoutGraft isRule pCout="cout"     followedBy PRINTing- stuff=PrintXP+ SemI-;
    PfCerrGraft isRule cerrGraft="cerr" followedBy PRINTing- stuff=PrintXP+ SemI-;
    ;
WardeD += PfCoutGraft;
WardeD += PfCerrGraft;
define
    PfCoutGraft ruleMethod=aCTionPrinT defer;
    PfCerrGraft ruleMethod=aCTionPrinT defer;
    ;
guard(WardeD);
/*  ---- END SCAFFOLDING GRAFT ----                                         */

define
    divBuf buffer;
    n1; n2; n3; n4;
    ;

print "PN ==== 1. cout / UNARMED ====":;
/*  RIGHT TODAY, and must stay right. These three are what native `cout`
    must produce byte for byte -- compare them against printFamily.target's
    PF-P-U-* rows, which are `print` with identical PrintXP.                */
cout "PN-C-U-def" 1 2 3:;
cout $"PN-C-U-dol" "joined":;
cout $"PN-C-U-und" _ "spaced":;

print "PN ==== 2. cerr / UNARMED ====":;
/*  WRONG TODAY: all three produce NOTHING, on either stream. The rule
    matches and consumes its text, then mis-sinks to opString and the value
    is discarded (CLAIM GRAM-2, CLAIM GRAM-4).
    MUST BECOME: three lines on stderr, byte-identical to the PN-C-U-* trio
    above but for the row name -- ONE spacing default across all four
    keywords (KANT-13). They belong in printFamilyNew.err.divergence, at
    THIS point in the run, ahead of the section-5 flush.                    */
cerr "PN-E-U-def" 1 2 3:;
cerr $"PN-E-U-dol" "joined":;
cerr $"PN-E-U-und" _ "spaced":;

print "PN ==== 3. ARMING DIVERSION -- THE LOAD-BEARING SECTION ====":;
printTO(divBuf);

/*  the control: `print` under an armed diversion goes to the buffer. This is
    correct today and stays correct -- it is KANT-23's other half.          */
print "PN-P-A-def" 1 2 3:;

/*  ############# THE LOAD-BEARING ROW #############
    WRONG TODAY: these three go INTO divBuf and reach nothing. They show up
    in printFamilyNew.err.divergence at the section-5 flush, mixed in with
    the diverted `print` line above them -- which is the defect, stated in
    bytes: grafted `cout` is divertible, and `cout` must not be.
    MUST BECOME: these three on STDOUT, HERE, between the "ARMING" and
    "RELEASED" markers, and ABSENT from the flush. `print` above them must
    still be in the flush. Both halves have to move together; if only one
    does, the diversion gate was widened rather than bypassed.
    ############################################### */
cout "PN-C-A-def" 1 2 3:;
cout $"PN-C-A-dol" "joined":;
cout $"PN-C-A-und" _ "spaced":;

/*  WRONG TODAY: nothing, as in section 2.
    MUST BECOME: three lines on stderr HERE, in statement order. `cerr` is
    not divertible either (KANT-23) -- an armed diversion must not capture
    them, so they must NOT appear in the section-5 flush.                   */
cerr "PN-E-A-def" 1 2 3:;
cerr $"PN-E-A-dol" "joined":;
cerr $"PN-E-A-und" _ "spaced":;

printTO(null);
print "PN ==== 4. DIVERSION RELEASED ====":;

print "PN ==== 5. FLUSHING THE DIVERSION BUFFER TO fd 2 ====":;
/*  Everything the diversion actually swallowed comes out on stderr here.
    TODAY that is PN-P-A-def plus all three PN-C-A-* rows.
    AFTER it must be PN-P-A-def ALONE.                                      */
divBuf modedOP "/dev/stderr";
closeFile(divBuf);

print "PN ==== 6. string, OMITTED FORM ====":;
/*  KANT-13's table closes the family at four and parenthesises `string (and
    its omitted form)`; KANT-15 rules that an OMITTED keyword must not change
    semantics, because the user who omitted it chose nothing.
    MEASURED TODAY, and it is neither -- see CLAIM GRAM-12:
      n1/n2  spelled `string`, one term and two terms  -- CORRECT
      n3     omitted, ONE term   -- value right, but a TRAILING SPACE that
                                    the spelled form has. Already a semantic
                                    difference under KANT-15.
      n4     omitted, TWO terms  -- GARBAGE. Not a truncated or mis-spaced
                                    answer: an uninitialised read. The token
                                    it prints appears NOWHERE in the tree.
    MUST BECOME: n3 == n1 and n4 == n2, byte for byte.
    NOT INCLUDED, and deliberately: the omitted form with a shortcut
    (`n = $"a" _ "b";`) does not parse and ABANDONS THE REST OF THE FILE at
    exit 0 (CLAIM GRAM-8). Putting it here would silently delete the
    sentinel. It is recorded in CLAIM GRAM-12 instead.                      */
n1 = string "PN-O-one";
print "spelled-1 [" n1 "]":;
n2 = string "PN-O" "two";
print "spelled-2 [" n2 "]":;
n3 = "PN-O-one";
print "omitted-1 [" n3 "]":;
n4 = "PN-O" "two";
print "omitted-2 [" n4 "]":;

print "PN SENTINEL -- run reached the end; if this line is absent the run TRUNCATED":;
stop();
